{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789494869,"nonce":"VgaS8Ciw7kWYBPGnKr5OqyI2GU8=","title":"A void return after a statement that branched ends the wrong block","message":"```python\ndef add(items, x, dest):\n    while True:\n        if not dest.startswith(\"-\"):\n            break\n        dest = dest[1:]\n    items.append(x)\nadd(items, 1, \"--n\")      # never returns\n```\n\nThe SSA builder puts a block's terminator on the continuation block when the block's last statement left it for one (an expression that branched, an inlined list primitive with its growth check): the valued `return` did so, `Jump` did so, but a `return` of nothing and the implicit return at a function's end set their terminator on the original block, which the statement's own branch had already taken over. The continuation kept the terminator its block was created with, a jump into the loop body here: the append ran, then the loop ran again, forever. With a valued return, or any statement after the append, it was fine, which is why the frontend's own tests never saw it.\n\nBoth returns now end the continuation block, as the others do. The typed CFG's \"a single expression statement in a function body is an implicit return\" no longer applies to an expression of no value.","files":null}]}